                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Finished playing this on my Youtube recently (link at end of review for anyone who wants to see a gameplay sample) and whilst I would recommend this one, I do so with some caveats.\r\n\r\nWelcome to Hanwell is a somewhat jank open-world horror game where you're thrown into a UK city infested with anomalous entities and under lockdown. Who's your player character? No idea; if the game told me I quickly forgot his name. Where did the anomalies come from? No idea; I feel like I left the game with more questions than answers. So the story isn't exactly the best. In essence, all you know is there's a creepy Doctor guy, and you need to collect six pieces of a citizenship card to access the city council HQ and defeat him.\r\n\r\nGraphically, the game looks decent enough - not amazing, but that's fine. A game doesn't need to look like the next big groundbreaking graphical experience in the world to be good. The sound design is also fine.\r\n\r\nIn terms of game mechanics, you basically explore the city, finding the primary locations where the citizenship card pieces are located. But there's also combat in this game, and in all honesty it's a real mixed bag: you can find melee weapons in certain places and use them to defend yourself. Some enemies aren't too much of a bother.\r\n\r\nBut then there's the teleporting mutant women enemies. Good god, they're annoying. When they show up, they teleport frequently - and usually close enough to you to take a swing before you can dodge. And it gets extremely annoying when they catch you in an enclosed area where you can't escape them easily, doubly so if you haven't found a melee weapon with which to defend yourself. A word of advice for anyone interested in playing: I'm pretty sure they mostly teleport in front of you. So point the camera in the direction opposite to where you're trying to go and hope for the best.\r\n\r\nAnd yet, despite the jank in this game, I kind of enjoyed it. It did have its moments where it was creepy and unsettling. So yes, I would recommend it - but bear in mind that what you're getting is far from Game-of-the-Year material.\r\n\r\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FXHcg3wdsZs&list=PLBFGPAeAm6nk",
